If two finite sets of raw lambda tokens are depth-consistent, every member of the first is depth-entailed by the second, and the first depth-entails a token, then the second depth-entails that token. This is Scott's entailment transitivity, obtained by cases on the token on the right.

The proof strengthens the claim to the maximum of the finset depth of the second set and the maximum of the finset depth of the first set and the raw depth of the token, and uses strong induction on that bound. The bottom token is the bottom clause for the second set. An atom entailment from the first set has empty function set and nonempty atom set. Entailment of one such atom by the second set forces that set into the same atom world with nonempty atom set, and the atom entailments of the underlying information system pass across by transitivity. A function-token entailment from the first set supplies a witness set of pairs from its function set. Each pair is entailed by the second set and contributes a witness subset of the function set of the second set. These witnesses are combined by induction on finite sets. The empty witness has empty output union, which is depth-consistent. At an inserted pair the accumulated witness is united with that pair's witness. Input tokens of the union are already entailed by the accumulated witness, or else they follow from the inductive hypothesis at a strictly smaller bound, comparing the depth of the input list, the depth of the source function token, and the depth of the witnessing function token. The input union is then depth-consistent by monotonicity and the entailment form of finitary union, and the nonempty function world of the second set makes the output union of the combined witness depth-consistent. Output tokens from either summand are weakened into that output union, by the function-world weakening when its function set is nonempty and by the bottom or atom weakening when that function set is empty. A function token on the empty side would place a function pair into that empty function set. The output clause for the original output list follows by the inductive hypothesis at a smaller bound, comparing the output-union depths of the two witnesses with the finset depths of the two sets and the raw depth of the output token. Emptiness of the function set of the input list passes to the input union of the combined witness, because an input function token would be entailed in that empty function world. The function-token clause for the second set is this combined witness.
